WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.628
How can a set of instructions help us find every factor?

00:00:04.278 --> 00:00:09.486
Today we are following an algorithm: a sequence of steps and decisions we can repeat.

00:00:09.706 --> 00:00:15.479
We will test candidates in order, record successful pairs, and decide when it is safe to stop.

00:00:16.129 --> 00:00:20.662
Our learning goal is to follow an algorithm to find factors systematically.

00:00:21.312 --> 00:00:23.062
Success criteria.

00:00:23.282 --> 00:00:26.075
I can repeat the test and record steps.

00:00:26.295 --> 00:00:29.339
I can record pairs only for exact division.

00:00:29.559 --> 00:00:32.445
I can stop before factor pairs repeat.

00:00:33.095 --> 00:00:35.835
Choose the target number and start with one.

00:00:36.055 --> 00:00:37.892
Test exact division.

00:00:38.112 --> 00:00:41.383
If it works, record the candidate and its partner.

00:00:41.603 --> 00:00:43.795
Increase the candidate by one.

00:00:44.015 --> 00:00:49.900
Stop when the candidate is greater than its possible partner, because pairs would repeat in reverse.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:55.402
For a small start, twelve has the pairs one and twelve, two and six, three and four.

00:00:56.052 --> 00:00:59.503
For thirty-six, test one, two, three and four.

00:00:59.723 --> 00:01:03.718
They produce partners thirty-six, eighteen, twelve and nine.

00:01:03.938 --> 00:01:06.970
Five leaves a remainder, so do not record it.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.950
Six divides exactly and pairs with six.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:16.317
The next candidate would be seven, already beyond its partner, so the search is complete.

00:01:16.537 --> 00:01:19.854
Record six only once when listing the factors.

00:01:20.504 --> 00:01:22.870
Apply the same steps to forty.

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:26.938
One pairs with forty, two with twenty, and four with ten.

00:01:27.158 --> 00:01:29.509
Three does not divide exactly.

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:31.999
Five pairs with eight.

00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:37.244
Six does not work, and by seven the candidate is greater than the partner it would need.

00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:42.515
The complete factors are one, two, four, five, eight, ten, twenty and forty.

00:01:43.165 --> 00:01:46.229
Finding one and forty is only the beginning.

00:01:46.449 --> 00:01:50.173
Keep increasing the test number and record each exact pair.

00:01:50.393 --> 00:01:55.890
Stop only when the test number passes its possible partner, so later pairs would repeat.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:02.606
For twenty-four, after recording four times six, should we also record six times four as a new pair?

00:02:06.606 --> 00:02:07.665
No.

00:02:07.885 --> 00:02:11.070
They use the same two factors in reverse order.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:14.673
A complete factor set includes each value once.

00:02:15.323 --> 00:02:21.348
An algorithm needs a starting point, a repeatable test, a way to record results, and a stopping rule.

00:02:21.568 --> 00:02:26.420
Follow every step so the search is complete without unnecessary repetition.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.048
Reflect on which model helped you and explain why.
